#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184976} :activity/id #uuid "abb54a6b-6262-52dd-afbc-0f162ea36991" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048838 :topic/id #uuid "c4ffa42c-7423-5c98-b774-781f0bbce72e" :topic/math-academy-id 3821} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184974 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184201 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184098 :question/id #uuid "9bdbb969-4745-503a-ba4b-fb07529b1b6c" :question/math-academy-id "e-9627"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f0567d5-4622-5c34-a448-0bc043fec12f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184460} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184821} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233384}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Function Given Graphically"} :step/id #uuid "effea6e4-63c7-5614-8184-2c75bc8926f8" :step/math-academy-id 36074} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184975 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184202 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184099 :question/id #uuid "3ab53f11-138b-5c41-ae90-0158c3fc8a5e" :question/math-academy-id "e-9636"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "55343d43-c173-5ba8-9a0e-58a76a282b7f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050790}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184458} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184459}] :knowledge-point/title "Plotting a Function"} :step/id #uuid "9a133363-5502-564b-9e1f-1936bbd31d3a" :step/math-academy-id 36105 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184977}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184976 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185323 :tutorial/content "We can plot a function $f(x)$ by setting $y=f(x)$.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's consider the function\n\n$$\nf(x) = x^2-1\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is any number taken from the set $\\{-2, -1, 0, 1,2 \\}$.\n\nFirst, we create a table containing the input values:\n\n| $x$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nGiven a value of $x$, we can find the corresponding $y$-value by substituting $x$ into the formula $y=x^2-1$. For example,\n\n- if $x=-2$ then $y=(-2)^2-1=3$,\n- if $x=-1$ then $y=(-1)^2-1=0$,\n- $\\cdots$\n\nContinuing in the same way, we get the following values:\n\n| $x$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $y$ | $3$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $3$ |\n\nFinally, we plot these $(x,y)$ pairs in the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/06/0623f7a3756288a982b2e26dbbf03f6ed924f2cdea495764bc78ed547efbbcc3.png)\n\nWe don't connect the points since $f(x)$ is defined only for values of $x$ contained within the set $\\{-2,-1,0,1,2\\}$.\n\nLet's now take a look at an example where $x$ is unrestricted." :tutorial/id #uuid "64dc2255-d088-5fec-900d-0b4006bbe575" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5912 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "e4765498-0187-5a99-9070-96e0233532f1" :step/math-academy-id 36149 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184975}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184977 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185324 :tutorial/content "Consider the function $y=f(x)$, shown below.\n\n![](images/fa/fa86c914aeedc2ff973d22228fb11902aa7246fa751bddb75746e517d97fa60b.png)\n\nWe can determine the values of this function at particular points by reading them off its graph.\n\n- Since the graph passes through the point $(-2,3)$, we know that $f(-2) = 3$.\n- Since the graph passes through the point $(-1,0)$, we know that $f(-1) = 0$.\n- Since the graph passes through the point $(0,-1)$, we know that $f(0) = -1$.\n\nNotice that the point $(2,3)$ is shown with an empty circle. This means that the function is **undefined** at the point where $x=2$." :tutorial/id #uuid "28431e0d-1c6b-51dc-ac06-829a76300f7a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5914 :tutorial/title "Evaluating Functions Using Graphs"} :step/id #uuid "5ef7df3c-689c-5e7c-9c3f-bb74fecad588" :step/math-academy-id 36151 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184974}}] :activity/title "Graphs of Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186184406}]}
